# Aqlamna (أقلامنا) — CodeWhale Constitution

## What this project is

Aqlamna is an Arabic-native interactive fiction engine: a scripting language (`.qalam`),
a parser + compiler, a browser runtime, and a visual node editor. Open source, GPL-3.0.
Domains: aqlamna.org (primary), aqlamna.com (redirect).

The authoritative design document is `ARABIC_IF_ENGINE_DESIGN.md` in the repo root.
READ IT before making architectural decisions. If a request conflicts with that
document, say so and stop — do not silently invent a different design.

`ARABIC_MASTERY.md` is the Arabic writing-quality rules corpus. It is a SOURCE OF
TRUTH DOCUMENT, not code. Never rewrite it, reformat it, or "clean it up".

## Hard rules

1. **Never hardcode Arabic writing rules into TypeScript.** Rules come from
   `ARABIC_MASTERY.md` via a build step that emits `rules.json`. If you find yourself
   typing an Arabic before/after pair into a `.ts` file, stop — it belongs in the
   markdown, and the build script must extract it.

2. **Do not edit files under `packages/core/tests/fixtures/`.** These are the
   acceptance tests. Expected output is written by the project owner. If a test fails,
   fix the code, never the expected result. If you believe a fixture is genuinely
   wrong, say so and stop.

3. **RTL is the default, not a feature.** `direction: rtl` everywhere. CSS logical
   properties only — `margin-inline-start`, never `margin-left`. No `left`/`right`.

4. **The runtime has zero dependencies.** `packages/runtime/` must compile to plain
   JavaScript that runs inside a single exported HTML file with no CDN calls, no npm
   imports at runtime, no framework. The editor may use React; the runtime may not.

5. **TypeScript strict mode on.** No `any` without a comment explaining why.

6. **Do not commit secrets.** No API keys, no `.env` files. Credentials arrive as
   environment variables set in the shell before launch.

7. **Ask before deploying.** `wrangler deploy`, `npm publish`, and `git push` to main
   require explicit confirmation each time.

8. **Never delete a file without asking.**

9. **Do not edit the governing documents.** `.codewhale/constitution`, `CLAUDE.md`,
   `PHASE1_SPEC.md`, `ARABIC_IF_ENGINE_DESIGN.md`, and `ARABIC_MASTERY.md` are owned by the
   project owner and his reviewer, not by you. Do not update the status block, tick off
   checkboxes, add gotchas, or "tidy" them — even when a step is finished and the update
   looks obviously correct. If one of them is wrong or contradicts a request, say so and
   stop. You own `packages/` and nothing else.

## Language and text handling

- All parser error messages must exist in BOTH Arabic and English.
- Variable names: strip tashkeel when resolving, so `كَلْب` and `كلب` are the same
  variable. Tashkeel in prose text is preserved exactly as written.
- Accept both Arabic-Indic (٠-٩) and Western (0-9) digits as numeric input.
- Recognise Arabic punctuation: `،` `؟` `؛` and both `""` and `«»`.
- `لا` is an operator ONLY inside `{ }` blocks and `*`/`+` choice conditions.
  Everywhere else it is ordinary prose text meaning "no".

## Code conventions

- ES modules, never CommonJS.
- Code comments in English. UI strings in Arabic (with English available).
- npm workspaces monorepo. Root folder is `aqlamna/`, npm scope is `@aqlamna/*`.
- Tests: Vitest for unit tests. Every parser or compiler change requires a test.
- Commit messages in English, imperative mood, one logical change per commit.

## Build order — do not skip ahead

Phase 1 is parser → tests green → compiler → tests green → runtime → HTML export.
Do NOT start the React editor, React Flow canvas, or CodeMirror integration until
Phase 1 acceptance tests all pass. If asked to jump ahead, remind the owner of this
rule and confirm before proceeding.

## Lessons from real mistakes on this project

These are not hypothetical. Each one happened during Phase 1.

**Never emit output you cannot represent.** A multi-branch conditional was compiled into a
single text node containing the raw branch syntax — the reader would have seen
`- الشجاعة < 3: ...` printed as prose. Nested `**` choices were flattened into siblings,
changing the story's logic. Only the first `= subsection` of a passage was emitted; the rest
vanished, leaving diverts pointing at nothing. All three produced valid-looking JSON and no
error. **Silent corruption is the worst failure mode in this codebase.** If you cannot
represent a construct, raise a coded error. Never fall back to printing source syntax as
content, and never drop a construct quietly.

**Prefer structural rules to pattern-sniffing.** Multi-branch conditionals were detected by
testing whether body lines start with `-`. That broke every conditional whose prose begins
with a dash — normal in Arabic dialogue. The fix was structural: multi-branch requires a
newline after the header. When you find yourself sniffing for a character, look for the
grammatical rule instead.

**A file that only prints is not a test.** `verify_tokenizer.ts` was reported as "edge case
tests." It contained zero assertions — a loop of `console.log`. It could never fail. Do not
describe something as a test unless it calls `expect()` and can fail.

**Do not report ✅ for anything you did not execute.** A status table claiming nine checks
passed was produced without running most of them. Report only what you ran, and say plainly
when you did not run something.

**Follow reporting instructions literally.** When asked to print a token dump and paste the
output, print it. A summary table instead of the requested evidence wastes a review cycle.

**Existing tests passing does not mean the feature works.** Fixtures 01-03 passed while three
language features were broken, because those fixtures were too small to exercise them. After
any change to the tokenizer, parser or compiler: **hand-write a NEW short story using only
syntax from `ARABIC_IF_ENGINE_DESIGN.md`, compile it, and report what happened.** That is how
all three silent bugs were found.

**You cannot see a browser. Never claim how something looks.** Asked to fix Arabic
symbols displaying in the wrong order, the report said "renders in correct visual order"
based on the presence of `dir="rtl"` and `perLineTextDirection` in the config. Nothing had
been rendered. The symbols were still reversed. **Config being present is not evidence that
config works.** For anything visual — layout, colour, text direction, whether an element
looks styled — state what you changed and say explicitly: "not visually verified — needs a
browser check." The owner has the browser. You do not.

**Read the function you are calling before assuming what it does.** A `<style>` element was
appended to a container, then `mount()` was called — and `renderScene()` opens with
`container.innerHTML = ""`, destroying it. Two files, one line apart in the call chain, never
read together. Before relying on a function's behaviour, open it.

**Never `git add .`** — add explicit paths. A blind `git add .` committed the owner's npm 2FA
recovery codes into a repo destined to be public. The `.gitignore` was correct; the glob was
not.

## Lessons from deployment day — 30 July 2026

The project shipped to a live URL for the first time. Every bug that day was the same
shape: **the source was right and the thing being served was not.** Read this section
before reporting anything as done.

**Source proves it exists. The artifact proves it shipped.** Three times in one evening a
change was correct in `packages/` and absent from the file on the CDN:

1. The `§1.16` paragraph fix landed in `parser.ts` at 17:21. `site/العطر_المفقود.html`
   was last written at 15:14 and was never re-exported. The live demo kept welding
   sentences together — `...لا شيء عليها."وصفة جدّتي ضاعت،"` — for two hours after the
   "fixed and verified" report.
2. The cream palette defined 46 CSS variables and **zero** components used them. 189
   hardcoded hex values remained. The page background changed; the top bar, settings
   panel, code editor and canvas stayed dark brown.
3. Save/load feedback was implemented in `packages/runtime/src/index.ts` and the exported
   story never loads that file. `build-runtime.mjs` carries its own hand-written
   `onSave`/`onLoad` that return `undefined`, so `showFeedback(toolbar, undefined)`
   displayed nothing.

Therefore: **a change is not done until you have grepped the artifact that ships and
reported the count.** Not the source file — the built bundle, the exported HTML, the
deployed URL. Report the number. "Verified" without a number means "I read my own code."

**When a value is duplicated, a test must assert the copies are equal.** The zero-runtime-
dependency rule (hard rule 4) forces the standalone export to inline its own copy of
things. That is allowed. What is not allowed is two copies with nobody watching them.
If you must duplicate, add a test that reads both and asserts they match — otherwise one
of them is already stale and no one knows which.

**Prefer deleting the duplicate to maintaining it.** Before writing a second
implementation of anything, say so explicitly and explain why importing the first one is
impossible. Hand-copying a handler into a build-script template string is how bug 3 above
happened.

**A green test that cannot fail on the bug is worse than no test.** A visual test asserting
one background colour passed while every other surface in the app was the wrong theme.
When you add a test for a change, ask: *if I reverted the change, would this test go red?*
If not, the test is decoration. Assert on every surface the change touches, not one.

**Check timestamps.** `dist/export.js` was two hours older than every sibling in the same
folder and nobody noticed. `ls -la` on the build output is a five-second check that would
have caught two of the three bugs above.

**Never swallow an error to keep the UI quiet.** `catch (_) { /* localStorage unavailable */ }`
turned a broken save into a silent no-op. If an operation can fail, the user must be told
it failed. Empty catch blocks are forbidden unless the comment explains what the user sees
instead.

**Do not touch files outside the scope you were given.** A commit that was meant to add an
AI textarea reverted six unrelated files edited by the reviewer — the company name fix, the
help links, the landing page docs section, the build wiring and a spec section. All of it
silently. Before committing, run `git status` and confirm every listed path is something
you deliberately changed. If a file you did not touch appears, stop and ask.

**The company's registered name is `المصير لبرامج وأنظمة الحاسوب`** — from the Companies
Control Department license. Not `المسير`, not `لبرمجيات`, not `لأنظمة البرمجيات`. Never
retype it from memory; copy it.

## Lessons from day two — 31 July 2026

**Environment variables do not survive a new session.** They live in the shell process that
set them. Every new terminal, and every CodeWhale started from one, begins empty. The load
script is `C:\Users\asus\load_env.ps1` and it is now dot-sourced from the PowerShell
`$PROFILE`, so a NEW window has everything. An OLD window opened before that change does not.
If a Cloudflare or provider call fails with "not set", the fix is a fresh terminal, not code.

**Do not claim you added something to package.json without reading it back.** `npm run deploy`
was reported as added twice, across two sessions, and was absent both times. The owner
discovered it by running the command. `grep` the file after writing it and paste the line.

**A checkpoint you did not execute is not a checkpoint.** Session 3 ended by printing the
command for the owner to run. The instruction was to generate one image and report four
numbers. Handing the run to the human and reporting "ready" is not the same as reporting a
result. If you cannot run it — no key, no network — say exactly that in one sentence.

**`build:all` exists because artifacts drifted three times in one evening.** Run it before any
deploy. Never regenerate one artifact by hand and assume the others followed.

**Measure the layout in EVERY view, not the one you changed.** A pane can have the right width
and contain nothing; a container can be 720px wide and 0px tall; a hidden pane can keep its
grid column and squeeze its neighbour to 4px. All three shipped as "verified" because one
combination was measured. Report x, width, height and computed `position` for every pane in
every combination, and add them up.

**Two elements only overlap if you compare them.** The editor painted over the player for
hours because it was `position: relative` and the player was `static` — positioned elements
paint after non-positioned ones regardless of DOM order. When panes sit side by side, assert
`paneA.x + paneA.width <= paneB.x` and assert every pane has the same `position` value.

**A React hook can blank the entire app.** `useReactFlow()` called outside `<ReactFlowProvider>`
throws during render; React unmounts everything and the page goes white while every HTTP check
still returns 200. **Read the browser console on the deployed page before reporting a UI change
done.** Console error count is a number — report it.

**Never truncate a source-of-truth document to fit a budget.** `build-mastery-prompt.mjs` kept
`slice(0, 25)` of 88 correction pairs, so every correction after roughly story 8 reached the
linter and never reached the AI — silently, for weeks, while `CLAUDE.md` claimed the opposite.
Raise the budget or fail loudly. Both generated artifacts now carry an md5 sidecar and a test
asserting the counts match.

**Do not print prices anywhere except a dated block in a spec.** "Free" was true about Together
in 2024 and false today; the owner discovered it when his account sat read-only. UI badges,
code comments and script output must say nothing about cost. Prices change; comments do not.

**Verify provider facts against the provider, and record the date.** Groq does vision INPUT
only and cannot generate images — it is constantly confused with xAI's Grok. Model display
names are not model strings: `FLUX.1 [schnell]` is a label, `black-forest-labs/FLUX.1-schnell`
is the identifier.

## When you are unsure

Say "I don't know" or "the design document doesn't specify this" and ask.
Do not guess at a design decision and build on the guess.
